
# Locally-built off-target 2-bit/seed indexes (large, regenerated on demand)
reference_data/offtarget_index/

# Secrets — never commit
.env
.env.*

# Dependencies / build output
node_modules/
dist/

# Local logs
*.log
local-server.log
deploy/local-server.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added after a `git add -A` swept 20MB of generated reference data and deploy/env-prod.js
# into a commit. The push was rejected, so nothing escaped -- but the only thing standing
# between that content and the remote was a server-side hook, which is not where this
# should be decided.
#
# Everything below is either GENERATED (rebuildable from a download or a script) or
# DEPLOYMENT-LOCAL (belongs to one machine). Nothing here is source.

# Downloaded / built reference genomes and their indexes. Large, and reproducible from the
# Ensembl and GENCODE URLs the loaders already carry.
#
# NOW IGNORED WHOLESALE, because naming the patterns could not keep up with the directory.
# It reached 30GB -- 22GB of off-target indexes and 7.4GB of variant databases -- while the
# rules below covered .fa.gz, .bgz and a couple of others. Every file not matched was one
# `git add -A` away from the remote, and that command gets run.
#
# `reference_data/*` rather than `reference_data/`: git will not look inside an excluded
# DIRECTORY, so a trailing-slash rule makes the re-inclusions below impossible. Excluding the
# contents keeps them workable.
#
# The five files already tracked are re-included by name. .gitignore does not untrack, so
# they would have kept working either way -- but an ignored-yet-tracked file is a trap for
# whoever next wonders why `git add` says nothing about their change to it. Named here, they
# behave normally.
#
# Adding a new reference file is now a deliberate act:
#   git add -f reference_data/<file>
# and it is worth asking first whether it belongs in git at all, or whether the loader that
# downloads it should simply be pointed at its URL.
reference_data/*
!reference_data/dog.annotation.gff3.gz
!reference_data/gencode.v46.basic.annotation.gff3.gz
!reference_data/gencode.v46.basic.annotation.sorted.gff3.gz
!reference_data/gencode.v46.basic.annotation.sorted.gff3.gz.tbi
!reference_data/yeast.annotation.gff3.gz

# The stray variant index a one-off run dropped at the repo root (00-common_all.vcf.gz.tbi).
# Scoped to .vcf.* rather than a blanket /*.tbi: there is ALREADY a tracked .tbi at the root
# (gencode.v46.basic.annotation.sorted.gff3.gz.tbi), and a rule that shadows a tracked file is
# a trap for whoever next wonders why their change to it is or is not being seen.
/*.vcf.gz
/*.vcf.gz.tbi

# Editor state: one developer's window layout, not the project's.
.vscode/

# The production client env. assets/env.js is EXCLUDED from the deploy rsync and maintained
# by hand on the server (see deploy/deploy.sh), so this local copy is a working artefact of
# one machine, and it carries tenant / client / redirect configuration that should be read
# before it is ever published. If it should be versioned, that is a deliberate act:
#   git add -f deploy/env-prod.js
deploy/env-prod.js

# Per-IP traffic reports pulled from the server (visitor addresses and emails)
deploy/geoblock/ip-activity-*.csv
deploy/geoblock/ip-activity-*.json
